From 9f13ebe12da708b6b1ea57c4e295ce481f14e120 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Romero <gusbromero@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:39:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(lib/smmuv3): do not require SMMU_IDR0.VMW

RMM only ever programs SMMU_R_CR0.VMW = 0 (TLB invalidations match VMID
tags exactly). When SMMU_IDR0.VMW == 0 that field is RES0, so writing 0
is equally valid and VMID wildcard matching is never used.

QEMU's SMMUv3 does not advertise VMW, so the hard requirement made RMM
disable Realm device assignment at boot:

  SMMUv3 @0x60050000: VMID wildcard-matching not supported
  SMMUv3: Failed to initialize driver
  Realm device assignment is not supported

Only log the absence of VMW instead of failing.
---
 lib/smmuv3/src/smmuv3.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/smmuv3/src/smmuv3.c b/lib/smmuv3/src/smmuv3.c
index 031b19a5..a25356e8 100644
--- a/lib/smmuv3/src/smmuv3.c
+++ b/lib/smmuv3/src/smmuv3.c
@@ -833,11 +833,16 @@ static int get_features(struct smmuv3_dev *smmu)
 		return -ENOTSUP;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * RMM programs SMMU_R_CR0.VMW to 0 (exact VMID matching). When
+	 * SMMU_IDR0.VMW == 0 that field is RES0, so writing 0 is still valid
+	 * and the behaviour is the same: VMID wildcard matching is not needed.
+	 * QEMU's SMMUv3 does not implement VMW.
+	 */
 	if ((idr0 & IDR0_VMW_BIT) != 0U) {
 		SMMU_DEBUG("\tVMW\n");
 	} else {
-		SMMU_ERROR(smmu, "VMID wildcard-matching not supported\n");
-		return -ENOTSUP;
+		SMMU_DEBUG("\tVMID wildcard-matching not supported\n");
 	}
 
 	if ((idr0 & IDR0_ASID16_BIT) != 0U) {
